<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mamluk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mamluk was an enslaved soldier, usually of Turkic or Caucasian origin, bought and trained for military service in Islamic states. Mamluks became central to the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>. In some cases they seized power themselves, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Mamluk dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mamluk.jpg" alt="Mamluk" width="290" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Hopfer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Mamelukes_with_lances_on_horseback.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
